    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is anchored by **Cal Raleigh** (79.31) and **Lawrence Butler** (73.46), who draw the most favorable matchups on the board. **Cal Raleigh** benefits significantly from a soft matchup against the left-handed Jeffrey Springs (4.84 xERA) and a park factor boost, while **Lawrence Butler** is positioned to feast on George Kirby\u2019s recent struggles, with his dynamic platoon advantage providing a +13% lift. **Dylan Beavers** also emerges as a high-upside play at 68.38; his aggressive placement in the leadoff spot and a +19% boost from facing the veteran Max Scherzer\u2014who carries an inflated 5.10 xERA\u2014makes him a critical asset for stolen base accumulation today. We are also leaning on **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (66.08) to provide veteran stability; his score remains high despite a middling matchup because his profile is immune to the usual platoon regressions, and his ability to capitalize on the slower delivery of Taj Bradley keeps his stolen base potential firmly in play.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was leaving **Daulton Varsho** (45.93) out of the starting nine. While his power-speed profile is enticing, his matchup against Drew Rasmussen (3.27 xERA) suppresses his ceiling enough that he falls just below the threshold of our utility slots, particularly with **Kyle Karros** (58.73) providing a cleaner path to production against the lefty Framber Valdez. We are monitoring **Bryce Harper** (48.50) closely; although he holds a solid xwOBA, he faces an elite arm in Chris Sale (2.81 xERA), which heavily dampens his projection. Every player today remains in \"Lineup Pending\" status, introducing a non-zero risk of late scratches; we have prioritized rostered hitters with favorable order placement, but if **Will Smith** or **Jonathan Aranda** shift downward in their respective orders, their value will see a swift correction before the first pitch.",
